course: everyday
lesson: 262
title: Prices, change, borrowing and lending
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Before you pay, you round each price and add them in your head, so you know whether you have enough before you reach the till; a price like $4.99 is almost $5, not $4.
explain: If a shop assistant gives you too much change, you tell them and give back the extra, because it is honest and the assistant may have to make up the difference.
explain: A loan is money you borrow and must pay back, often with extra called interest, so adults borrow carefully; borrowing from a parent and paying it back from pocket money is a small loan that teaches what debt means.
explain: You lend friends only a small amount you can afford to lose and agree when it will be paid back, because friends sometimes forget and money can spoil friendships.
explain: A boy in Bristol is cornered by an older child who demands his money; he hands it over because he feels unsafe, then tells a teacher straight away, because this is bullying or theft and adults must stop it.
fact: If you get too much change, you tell the shop assistant and give back the extra.
fact: To estimate a total, you round each price and add them in your head.
fact: A price like $4.99 is almost $5; shops use such prices to make things seem cheaper.
fact: At an ice-cream van or tuck shop (a small school shop for snacks) you check the price list first and hold your money ready.
fact: You lend a friend only a small amount you can afford to lose and agree when it will be paid back.
fact: If a friend has not paid you back, you remind them kindly and ask a parent for advice if it continues.
fact: When you borrow from a parent, you agree how and when you will pay it back from pocket money.
fact: A loan is money you borrow and must pay back, often with extra called interest.
fact: If an older child demands your money, you may give it if you feel unsafe, then tell a trusted adult straight away.
fact: Taking money from a parent's purse without asking is stealing, even at home.
exercise: The shop assistant gives you $5 too much change. What do you do? | Tell the assistant and give back the extra | Keep it, as it was their mistake | Keep it and buy sweets | Leave quickly before they notice
exercise: Why should you give back extra change? | It is honest, and the assistant may have to pay it back | The shop will chase you down the street | Extra change is fake money | You will get a reward from the shop every single time you do it
exercise: You want items costing $1.95, $3.10 and $2.90, and you have $8. How can you check quickly? | Round to $2, $3 and $3 and add: about $8 | Add only the first two prices | Hand over the $8 and hope that it is enough for everything | Guess from how full the basket is
exercise: A video game costs $29.99. About how much is that? | Almost $30 | About $29 | About $3 | Nearer to $20 than to $30
exercise: Why do shops use prices like £4.99? | To make things seem cheaper than they are | Because 99p coins are common | Because tax rounds them down | Because shops are not allowed to use prices like £5
exercise: The ice-cream van arrives. What should you do before ordering? | Check the price list and have your money ready | Order first and find out the price later at the window | Ask for the biggest ice cream | Push to the front of the queue
exercise: A friend asks to borrow $20 for a game. What is wise? | Lend only what you can afford to lose; agree when it comes back | Lend all your savings to keep the friend | Lend it without talking about paying back | Ask for $40 back next week, to make it worth your while lending it
exercise: Your friend borrowed $2 a month ago and has not paid it back. What do you do first? | Remind them kindly that it is owed | Shout at them in the playground | Take $2 from their bag | Tell everyone they are a thief
exercise: You want a $15 kite now, and your dad lends you the money. What is right? | Agree how and when you will pay it back from pocket money | Forget about it, since it is your dad | Pay it back only if he reminds you | Ask him to lend you more next week for a bigger kite to go with it
exercise: What is a loan? | Money you borrow and must pay back, often with interest | Money someone gives you as a present | Money you find on the street | Money a bank prints for you
exercise: An older child at the bus stop says, "Give me your money or else." What should you do? | Give it if you feel unsafe, then tell a trusted adult at once | Fight them to keep your money | Keep it secret so they do not get angry | Promise to bring more money tomorrow if they leave you alone today
exercise: You see a $10 note in your mum's purse and want a magazine. What is right? | Ask her first; never take money without permission | Take it, as family money is shared | Take it and pay it back secretly later, before she counts | Take only half of it
exercise: Why do adults borrow money carefully? | Borrowing costs extra money in interest | Banks never let anyone borrow any money at all | Borrowed money disappears after a week or so | Loans are only for children and not adults
exercise: Your friend never pays back the money you lent him, even after a reminder. What next? | Ask a parent for advice | Stop speaking to him forever | Take something of his instead | Lend him more so he feels guilty
test: A girl in Houston pays with a $20 bill for a $6 lunch and gets $24 back. What has happened, and what should she do? | She got $10 too much, so she gives it back | She got the right change | She got $4 too much, so she keeps it as luck | The shop owes her even more money
test: A boy in Leeds has £5. He wants a comic at £2.99 and a drink at £1.49. Can he afford both? | Yes, the total is about £4.50 | No, the total is about £6, which is too much | Yes, with £3 left over to spend | No, he needs about £8 for the two
test: A sign says "Only $9.99!" What is the honest way to think of this price? | It is basically $10 | It is about $9, as you drop the cents | It is a lot less than $10 | It is half price, a real bargain
test: A girl lends her friend her whole $50 savings, and the friend loses it. What lesson does this show? | Lend only what you can afford to lose | Always lend everything you have to friends | Friends never lose or spend money they borrow | Lending money to friends always ends friendships
test: Your mum lends you $10 for a gift and you agree to pay back $2 a week from pocket money. How many weeks will it take? | 5 weeks | 2 weeks | 10 weeks | 8 weeks
test: Your parents borrow money from a bank to buy a car. Why will they pay back more than they borrowed? | The bank charges interest on the loan | The car gets more expensive over time | Banks always make mistakes | The car comes with free petrol for a year
test: An older boy in Melbourne threatens a younger boy and takes his lunch money. Why must the younger boy tell an adult? | It is bullying or theft, and adults must stop it | He will get double his money back | The older boy will apologise at once | It is his own fault for having money with him at school
test: A boy in Atlanta takes $5 from his grandpa's wallet without asking and plans to put it back later. What is true? | It is stealing, even at home and even if he returns it | It is fine because it is family | It is fine if it is less than $10 | It is only borrowing, so nobody minds as long as the money is put back
test: At the school tuck shop there is a long queue. What helps you and everyone else? | Checking the prices and having your money ready | Deciding what to buy at the counter | Counting your coins slowly at the front | Asking the price of every item
test: lady gave me 5 dollars change but it should be 3. sweet! | Tell her and give back the extra | Keep it, her mistake | Run before she notices | Buy more sweets with it from the same shop
test: 3 things, $1.99, $2.99 and $4.99, roughly how much, no calculator | About 10 dollars | About 7 dollars | About 15 dollars | About 4 dollars
test: big kid says gimme your money or else. i'm scared | Give it if you feel unsafe, then tell a trusted adult | Fight him for it | Keep it secret forever | Run into the road to escape from him, cars will stop
test: mum's purse is right there, i'll just take 2 quid, she won't mind | No, taking money without asking is stealing | Yes, it's family money | Yes, if it's only coins | Only if you put it back next week before she checks
explain: A boy in England called Jack paid with a £20 note and did not count his change; he had been given £5 too little and only noticed at home, when it was too late to prove it.
explain: A girl in California lent her new headphones to a boy she hardly knew; he never gave them back and she saved for months to replace them.
fact: Counting your change before you leave the till is the only time a mistake is easy to fix.
fact: Things lent to people you do not know well are often lost, so lend only what you can manage without, to people you trust.
test: got my change, just shove it in my pocket | Count it before you leave the till | Fine, tills are never wrong | Count it when you get home | Ask the next customer in the queue to check it for you
test: kid i barely know wants to borrow my new headphones for a week | Say no, or lend only what you can lose | Lend them, he'll give them back | Lend them and ask him for money | Give them to him to keep, then you can't lose them
test: Why could Jack not get his missing £5 back? | He only noticed after he had left the shop | The shop keeps all mistakes | £5 is too little to matter | He was given too much
test: did u hear an older kid made a year 3 (a child of about seven) hand over his lunch money behind the shops? what should a kid in that spot do? | Give it if unsafe, then tell a trusted adult at once | Fight him for it | Never tell anyone | Bring more money next time so he leaves you alone after that
test: apparently the shop gave a kid 20 dollars too much change and he kept it and nobody ever found out lol. what's the true lesson? | Tell the assistant and give back the extra | Keep it since it was their mistake | Spend it quickly | Only give back big amounts, small ones don't matter to shops
test: my mate says $4.99 is basically $4. right? | No, it's almost $5; the price just seems cheaper | Yes, round down always | Yes, the 99 cents don't count | No, it's about $6
exam: Identify the approximate value of a price of $4.99. | Nearly five dollars | Approximately four dollars | About six dollars, if you round it | Exactly four dollars and nine cents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a loan? | Money borrowed that must be repaid, often with interest | Money given as a gift | Money that need never be repaid | Money earned by working
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice upon receiving excess change. | One informs the assistant and returns the extra | One retains it as good fortune | One spends it at once | One returns only half of it to the assistant
exam: Ascertain the moment at which an error in one's change is most easily corrected. | Before leaving the till | On the following day, at the counter | After arriving home | One week later
